\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li284\ri444\kerning1\b DETAILS METHODS OF USE AND OPERATING PARAMETERS:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ri444\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri444\qc\tx9498\b0\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri444\tx4275\tx9498 The following describes in detail all the parameters, the options of the program and the controls\par
to modify it, displayed in the GUI:\tab\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri444\tx9498\par
\b Log analysis: control "Create log"\par
\b0 The control "Create log" informs the analysis engine if the user wants to create a log.\par
By default, the control is unchecked. If you check the box, it will create a trace log written\par
to the "log.txt" file in the installation folder.\par
The application measures and displays the execution time of the analysis, which is useful for\par
the benchmark. Example: Compare the execution time of analysis, using certain parameters, \par
with the time obtained with a different version of the program or by another user with a\par
different PC.\par
The log can be found over at the time, all the parameters of the analysis and the results obtained.\par
The log is written in "\b log.txt\b0 " text file  . "\b log.txt\b0 " file, always must be present in the installation\par
folder.\par
\par
\b Analysis \ldblquote Full Montecarlo\rdblquote  or "Limited": control "Limited analysis"\par
\b0 The control "\b limited analysis\b0 " tells the analysis engine if the user wants to perform an analysis of\par
the type "Full Montecarlo" or "limited".\par
By default, the control is unchecked.\par
If you check the box, the analysis will be of the type "limited".\par
If the control is not checked will be performed an analysis "Full Monte Carlo".\par
In "Full Montecarlo" mode, the program plays games from the position in analysis until the end.\par
In other words, until when the game ends draw or won by a colour.\par
The results are displayed as: number of games won, draws, lost and percentage of the maximum\par
possible result obtained.\par
In "limited" mode, the program plays the games only for a certain number of moves, number\par
established by the user ("limit move"). Reached the "limit move" the program stores the evaluation\par
of the position, expressed in hundredths of pawn (cp.), as a normal engine. Then the game is over.\par
The results are shown as mean algebraic of the evaluations stored and percentage of maximum\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri-164\tx9498 possible result obtained.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri444\tx9498\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li142\ri444\tx9498\b  Limit moves (if analysis mode = limited): control "n.limit moves"\par
\b0  If the user chooses to perform the analysis in "limited" mode, checking control "\b limited \par
 analysis\b0 ", the program imposed as \ldblquote limit move" the value 15. The user can change this value by the \par
 control "\b n.limit moves\b0 ".\par
 The "limit moves" is the maximum number of moves for each game. (Of course if the game doesn't\par
 end before, to the victory of one colour or stall). The maximum value. is 40 moves, minimum 2.\par
 \par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri444\tx9498\par
\b Note-concept parameters "LOW" and "normal" \par
\b0 The user has the ability to "split" the games in two parts.\par
In the sense:  the user can choose to apply the parameters of analysis that will be used throughout the\par
game, except the first move. In this first part of the game (first moves), consisting of a number of \par
moves chosen by the user (max. 21), you can apply different parameters from those used throughout\par
the rest of the game. This first section of the game is identified as the "LOW" and the parameter that\par
determines the length of moves is: "number LOW moves" (max. 21). All the parameters applied to this\par
initial part of the game will have the file name extension "LOW".\par
For example, if I set "n. LOW moves" = 5, "LOW depth" = 9 and "depth\rdblquote  = 7, the first 5 moves of the\par
game will be analyzed to depth 9, the remaining moves will be analyzed  to depth 7. This is valid for all\par
parameters shown below.\par
Note: we speak of "game, but obviously the division will be applied to all the matches of the analysis\par
session.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri444\par
\b Depth: control "Depth"\par
\b0 The control sets the analysis depth used from analysis engine.\par
At startup, the value is set by default to 7.\par
The analysis depth maximum is 21, the minimum and 2, expressed in half moves as the normal engine.\par
\par
\b Value Multi pv: control "Multi pv"\par
\b0 The control sets the number of variants to be considered for move (max. 21).\par
At startup, the default value is set to 3.\par
Example: if you set the value multi pv = 3, for each move of the game the program will considers 3\par
moves, randomly choosing one of it.\par
The value of "Multi pv" maximum is 21, but it is advisable to choose a value less than 6. This is \par
because in addition to issues of time, often the engine of the program does not generate a number\par
of variations too high or are not even possible these variations. In fact, increasing the "Multi pv" \par
value too much,  user may not notice a corresponding increase of time, just because are not generated\par
all variants.\par
\par
\b Value Delta: control "Delta"\par
\b0 The control sets the value of the "Delta" in hundredths of pawn (min. 1, max. 1200).\par
At startup, the value is set to 100 by default.\par
Delta is the maximum difference of evaluation of a move compared to the best move.\par
For best move means the move is rated the highest value in hundredths of pawn by the analysis engine\par
of the program.\par
Example: if you set the value Delta = 90, and the best move for the colour to moves is evaluated \par
+100 cp (+1 pawn), will be taken into consideration all the moves evaluated between +100 and 10 cp\par
(+ 1 and 0.1 pawn). With Delta = 90 if the best move for the colour to move currency is -100, will be\par
considered with all the moves between -100 and -190 assessment cp (-1 and -1.9 pawn).\par
\par
\b Number LOW moves: control "n.LOW moves"\par
\b0 The control sets the length in number of moves of the first part of the game, in which you can apply\par
parameters other than those used in the rest of the game (max. 21).\par
At startup, the value is set by default to 7.\par
Example: if we set the parameter "number LOW moves" = 5, for the first 5 moves of the game will\par
apply the parameters of analysis "LOW". The remaining moves of the game will be analyzed with the\par
"normal" ranges.\par
\par
\b LOW depth: control "LOW depth"\par
\b0 The control sets the depth of analysis to apply to the first moves ( n. first moves = n. LOW moves).\par
At startup, the value is set by default to 5.\par
Example: if you set the parameter "LOW Depth" = 9 and \ldblquote Number LOW moves\rdblquote  = 5, during the\par
first 5 moves of the game, the engine will work in depth = 9, the rest of the game engine will work\par
on the depth of analysis set with  the parameter "depth\rdblquote .\par
\par
\b Value \ldblquote LOW multi pv\rdblquote : control "LOW multi pv"\par
\b0 The control sets the number of variants to be considered for move (max. 21) during the initial part \par
of the game. At startup, the default value is set to 4.\par
Example: if you set the value of " LOW multi pv" = 3 and the \ldblquote number LOW moves\rdblquote  = 5, during \par
the first 5 moves of the game, the program will consider three moves (variants) to move. In the \par
remainder of the game the program will consider a number of variations to the value = "multi pv".\par
\b\par
Value \ldblquote LOW  delta\rdblquote :\b0  \b control \ldblquote LOW delta\rdblquote\par
\b0 The control sets the value of the "LOW delta" in hundredths of pawn (min. 1, max. 1200).\par
At startup, the value is set to 150 by default.\par
"LOW delta" is the maximum difference of evaluation of a move compared to the best move,\par
but only during the initial part of the game.\par
For best move means the move is rated the highest value in hundredths of pawn by the analysis\par
engine of the program.\par
Example: if you set the value "LOW delta" = 120, and "LOW number moves\rdblquote  = 5, when the\par
engine chooses the first 5 moves, if the best move for the colour to moves is evaluated +100 cp\par
(+1 pawn), will be taken into consideration all the moves evaluated between 100 and -20 cps \par
(1 and -0,2 pawns). In the remaining moves of the game the engine will consider the moves inside\par
the range planned with the parameter \ldblquote delta\rdblquote .\par
\par
\b Hash: control \ldblquote Hash\rdblquote\b0\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri-22 The control sets the value of the memory allocated for the Hash table of the analysis engine.\par
At startup, the value is set to the default of 256Mb. The minimum value is 32MB, the maximum\par
value is 1024MB\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\li225\ri444\par
\b Threads: control \ldblquote Threads\rdblquote\b0\par
The control sets the value of the maximum number of threads/cores used by the analysis engine.\par
At startup, the default value is set to 2. The minimum value is 1, the maximum value is 16.\par
\pard\li225\ri444\sl240\slmult0 Note: The control is passive in the sense that merely informs to the engine of analysis the number\par
of threads chosen by the user. It's the analysis engine (when is open the console window) that\par
detects the number of cores available in system hardware. Then if the number of threads set by\par
the user, is higher of the cores number of the system, will never be considered. Example: If the\par
program run on a dual-core PC and the user sets the "\b Threads\b0 " to 4, the analysis engine \kerning0 will\par
always use max. 2 cores.\kerning1  While if you have a quadcore and sets the "\b Threads\b0 " to 2, will be used\par
only two of the four available cores.\par
